---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="FIELD OPERATIONS" description="Authorized operational use cases for GhostMesh.">
  <div class="bg-grid" aria-hidden="true"
    style="position:fixed;inset:0;background-image:linear-gradient(rgba(0,212,255,0.025) 1px,transparent 1px),linear-gradient(90deg,rgba(0,212,255,0.025) 1px,transparent 1px);background-size:64px 64px;z-index:0;"></div>

  <div class="corner corner--tl" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="corner corner--tr" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="corner corner--bl" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="corner corner--br" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="sys-bar sys-bar--top">
    <span class="sys-text">GHOSTMESH &gt; FIELD OPERATIONS</span>
    <a href="/" class="return-link">← BACK</a>
  </div>

  <main class="content-panel">
    <p class="classification">CLASSIFICATION: AUTHORIZED USE ONLY / LAB AND FIELD SCOPE</p>
    <h1>FIELD OPERATIONS</h1>
    <p style="font-size:0.7rem;color:var(--text-dim);letter-spacing:0.15em;margin-bottom:2.5rem;">
      What this hardware does in the field. Scope applies to owned systems and authorized engagements only.
    </p>

    <h2>GRID-DOWN COMMUNICATIONS</h2>
    <span class="tag tag--active">AVAILABLE NOW</span>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem;">
      When cell towers are down, WiFi is gone, and internet infrastructure has failed,
      GhostMesh keeps a team coordinated. LoRa mesh radio requires no external infrastructure —
      each node relays traffic for every other node in range. Three nodes can cover
      kilometers of terrain.
    </p>
    <p>
      Built-in profiles cover common field status: check-in, moving, hold position, medical
      needed, shelter in place. Custom profiles via SD card cover operation-specific callouts.
    </p>

    <h2>SEARCH AND RESCUE</h2>
    <span class="tag tag--active">AVAILABLE NOW</span>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem;">
      The Hiking/SAR profile provides coordination messages designed for search and rescue
      operations: trail status, summit reached, camp reached, need water, need medical,
      turning back. Range and battery life make this practical for multi-day operations.
    </p>

    <h2>AUTHORIZED RED TEAM COORDINATION</h2>
    <span class="tag tag--active">AVAILABLE NOW</span>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem;">
      During authorized assessments where cellular and WiFi may be monitored or unavailable,
      GhostMesh provides an out-of-band coordination channel. Traffic travels over
      Meshtastic AES-256 mesh — no cell towers, no WiFi, no logs on infrastructure you
      don't control.
    </p>
    <p>
      Private channel required for operational use.
      The default channel uses a public key visible to every Meshtastic node worldwide.
      Generate a private channel with a random key before deployment.
    </p>

    <h2>DEAD-DROP SURVEILLANCE</h2>
    <span class="tag">PLANNED — PHASE 10–11</span>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem;">
      Deploy the Heltec backpack at a dead-drop location. The node monitors the area
      and broadcasts alerts over LoRa when the zone is disturbed — proximity detection
      (HC-SR04), physical disturbance (tilt switch), or case opening (photoresistor).
    </p>
    <p>
      The backpack operates fully unattended. Arm it on deployment with the physical
      slide switch. Arm or disarm remotely from ~10m via IR. Receive alerts on your
      Flipper miles away. If discovered: one action wipes all encryption keys.
    </p>

    <h2>OPSEC — ENCRYPTION</h2>
    <table class="data-table" aria-label="Encryption coverage by segment">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>SEGMENT</th><th>ENCRYPTED</th><th>RISK</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Flipper → Heltec (UART)</td>
          <td style="color:var(--red);">No (Phase 14 planned)</td>
          <td>High — physical access</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Heltec → Mesh (default channel)</td>
          <td style="color:var(--terminal);">AES-256 — public key</td>
          <td>Medium — visible to all nodes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Heltec → Mesh (private channel)</td>
          <td style="color:var(--terminal);">AES-256 — private key</td>
          <td>Low — opaque to outsiders</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>SCOPE STATEMENT</h2>
    <p>
      All features described here apply only to systems you own or have explicit written
      authorization to test, in controlled lab or field environments, and for authorized
      penetration testing engagements or open-source security research.
    </p>
    <p>
      No malware, unauthorized access, credential theft, destructive payloads, or
      interference with licensed radio spectrum is implemented, planned, or accepted.
    </p>

    <div style="margin-top:2.5rem;">
      <p style="font-size:0.75rem;color:var(--text-dim);">
        Full use case documentation at
        <a href="https://github.com/ComplacencyKillz/GhostMesh/blob/main/docs/red-team-lab-use-cases.md"
           style="color:var(--blue);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid var(--text-ghost);"
           target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          github → red-team-lab-use-cases.md →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>

  <div class="sys-bar sys-bar--bottom">
    <span class="sys-text">ghostmesh.info</span>
    <span class="sys-text" id="ts"></span>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  const el = document.getElementById('ts');
  const tick = () => { if (el) el.textContent = new Date().toISOString().replace('T',' ').slice(0,19)+' UTC'; };
  tick(); setInterval(tick, 1000);
</script>
